From 47100b589d99e5f8eedf63334c13eaaf4092724c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 14:48:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall lists leave nvram; policy routing: multi-address
 source rules, self-healing chain (field feedback)

Field report (2026-08-22, Policy Routing + Firewall objects):

1. A 40-domain object "would not save and apply"; a few domains at a time
   eventually did. The firewall engine's eight lists (obj grp svc zone zpol
   rules edef nat) were still kernel-nvram tuples under the 1 KB cap
   (v2.6.3 / v2.6.7 all over again) while httpd allowed 8 KB, so the set
   silently failed. They now use the same file store as the routing list:
   shared/reaper_jfile.c reaper_fw_list_get(key) resolves the staged draft
   (/tmp/reaper_fw/draft/<key>, written by httpd `save`) > the confirmed
   list (/jffs/reaper_fw/lastgood/<key>, written only by confirm) > the
   legacy nvram value; rfw_migrate_lists() seeds the snapshot from nvram
   once at boot and clears the nvram copies. The rollback script no longer
   `nvram set`s (it could not restore a list over 1 KB either): dropping the
   draft is the rollback. rfw_write_lastgood snapshots all eight BEFORE it
   invalidates the marker. With the engine OFF nothing would ever have
   persisted a draft, so a policy-routing confirm now promotes the object /
   group lists (only those two - additive - and never while the engine's
   own change is armed).
2. A pasted list of IPs "blanked the UI": the editor took the value raw, so
   newlines reached the rule record and the page's inline SSI string. A
   source rule now takes a list of IPv4/IPv6 addresses or CIDRs (one per
   line or comma-separated, 64 max), validated per entry in the page and
   again in rc; the engine emits one mark rule per entry and one WireGuard
   bypass entry per entry; a bad entry drops only itself.
3. After a reboot some REAPER_PBR mark rules were missing while the ip
   rules were present (a `-m set` rule fails when its ipset is not there
   yet). apply.sh now counts what it meant to load (expect) and what failed
   (failcount, logged); the rwatch tick compares the live chain with expect
   and re-runs the script (candidate while armed) once per 2 minutes - before
   the boot grace, because boot is when it happens.

Diag v1.3.3 counts the lists from the files, shows the store in use, the
staged draft, and expected/live/failed mark rules; WARN if a list is still
in nvram after migration.

Host tests: generated script under mock iptables/ip (multi-entry source
rule -> 2 mark rules + 2 bypass entries, pbr_rule accounting, teardown
clears the counters); commit-confirm state machine (migration, 20 rules,
promote called on confirm); page JS x 25 dicts; langcheck 0.
